steps.body:
In a sorted array, two pointers find a pair with a given sum in one pass. Start `left` at the first item and `right` at the last. If their sum is too small, only moving `left` up can make it bigger. If it is too big, move `right` down. Each move rules out one item for good, so the loop runs at most n times instead of checking all n * n pairs.

```javascript
const nums = [1, 3, 4, 6, 9];
const target = 10;
let left = 0;
let right = nums.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
  const sum = nums[left] + nums[right];
  if (sum === target) {
    break;
  }
  if (sum < target) {
    left++;
  } else {
    right--;
  }
}
console.log(left, right, nums[left] + nums[right]); // 0 4 10
```

After the loop, `left < right` means the pair was found, and `left === right` means no pair adds up to the target. Problems that number positions from 1 want `left + 1` and `right + 1`.

**The gotcha:** this only works on sorted input. Sort numbers with `(a, b) => a - b` first, and if you need the original positions, sort `[value, index]` pairs instead of bare values.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [budgetLine, pricesLine] = input.trim().split("\n");
const budget = Number(budgetLine);
const prices = pricesLine.trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number);

let left = 0;
let right = prices.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
  const sum = prices[left] + prices[right];
  console.log(`try ${prices[left]} + ${prices[right]} = ${sum}`);
  if (sum === budget) {
    break;
  }
  if (sum < budget) {
    left++;
  } else {
    right--;
  }
}

if (left < right) {
  console.log(`positions ${left + 1} and ${right + 1}: ${prices[left]} + ${prices[right]} = ${budget}`);
} else {
  console.log(`no pair adds to ${budget}`);
}

