steps.body:
To filter an array in place, without building a second array, use two indexes that move the same way. `read` visits every item. `write` marks where the next item you keep goes. `write` never passes `read`, so nothing gets overwritten before it is read.

```javascript
const nums = [3, 0, 5, 0, 0, 7];
let write = 0;
for (let read = 0; read < nums.length; read++) {
  if (nums[read] !== 0) {
    nums[write] = nums[read];
    write++;
  }
}
nums.length = write; // drop the leftovers
console.log(nums.join(" ")); // 3 5 7
```

The kept items stay in their order, and the only extra memory is two numbers. Setting `length` shortens the array in place.

On a **sorted** array, the same loop removes duplicates: an item is new when it differs from the last one kept, `nums[write - 1]`. Comparing with the kept part, not just the previous item, is what lets you build rules such as "keep each value at most twice".

**The gotcha:** don't `splice` items out while looping over the same array. Every `splice` shifts the rest down, which costs O(n) each time and makes the loop skip the item that slid into the current index.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const nums = input.trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number);

let write = 0;
for (let read = 0; read < nums.length; read++) {
  if (write === 0 || nums[read] !== nums[write - 1]) {
    nums[write] = nums[read];
    write++;
  }
}
nums.length = write;

console.log(`${nums.length} unique: ${nums.join(" ")}`);

