practice.solution:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [kLine, text] = input.trim().split("\n");
const k = Number(kLine);
const s = text.trim();

const counts = new Map();
let left = 0;
let best = 0;
let bestLeft = 0;
for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
  counts.set(s[right], (counts.get(s[right]) ?? 0) + 1);
  while (counts.size > k) {
    const out = s[left];
    counts.set(out, counts.get(out) - 1);
    if (counts.get(out) === 0) {
      counts.delete(out);
    }
    left++;
  }
  if (right - left + 1 > best) {
    best = right - left + 1;
    bestLeft = left;
  }
}

console.log(`${best}: ${s.slice(bestLeft, bestLeft + best)}`);

steps.body:
A **prefix sum** array stores running totals: `prefix[i]` is the sum of the first `i` items, starting from `prefix[0] = 0`. Build it once in O(n), and then the sum of any range `nums[l]` through `nums[r]` is one subtraction, `prefix[r + 1] - prefix[l]`.

```javascript
const nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5];
const prefix = [0];
for (const x of nums) {
  prefix.push(prefix[prefix.length - 1] + x);
}
console.log(prefix.join(" ")); // 0 3 4 8 9 14
console.log(prefix[4] - prefix[1]); // 6, that is nums[1] + nums[2] + nums[3]
```

The extra 0 in front means a range that starts at index 0 needs no special case. Each range question then costs O(1) instead of a loop.

Sometimes you don't need the array at all. With the total known, a running sum as you walk gives everything to the left of index `i`, and `total - running - nums[i]` gives everything to the right.

**The gotcha:** `prefix` has one more entry than `nums`, so the sum through index `r` ends at `prefix[r + 1]`. Off by one, the sum is wrong by one item, with no error.

Running sums also count the subarrays that add up to `k`: one ends here exactly when an earlier running sum equals `sum - k`. Keep a Map of how often each running sum has appeared, starting with 0 seen once:

```javascript
const nums = [1, 2, 1, 2];
const k = 3;
const seen = new Map([[0, 1]]);
let sum = 0;
let count = 0;
for (const x of nums) {
  sum += x;
  count += seen.get(sum - k) ?? 0;
  seen.set(sum, (seen.get(sum) ?? 0) + 1);
}
console.log(count); // 3
```

Look up `sum - k` before recording `sum`. Unlike a sum window, this works with negative numbers.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [first, ...queries] = input.trim().split("\n");
const steps = first.trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number);

const prefix = [0];
for (const x of steps) {
  prefix.push(prefix[prefix.length - 1] + x);
}

for (const q of queries) {
  const [l, r] = q.trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number);
  console.log(`days ${l}-${r}: ${prefix[r] - prefix[l - 1]}`);
}

